This task involves refactoring the large Dashboard.vue component to improve maintainability, readability, and reusability:

1. Analyze the current Dashboard.vue component:
   - Identify distinct UI sections and responsibilities (charts, tables, filters, etc.)
   - Map out data flow and state management
   - Document business logic that can be extracted
   - Identify reusable patterns for potential composables

2. Create a component architecture plan:
   - Design a hierarchy of smaller components
   - Define clear interfaces between components
   - Identify shared state requirements
   - Document component responsibilities

3. Extract UI components:
   - Create smaller, focused components for each UI section:
     ```vue
     <!-- Example: DashboardMetricsCard.vue -->
     <template>
       <div class="metrics-card">
         <h3>{{ title }}</h3>
         <div class="metrics-value">{{ formattedValue }}</div>
         <trend-indicator :value="trend" />
       </div>
     </template>

     <script setup>
     import { computed } from 'vue'
     import TrendIndicator from './TrendIndicator.vue'

     const props = defineProps({
       title: { type: String, required: true },
       value: { type: Number, required: true },
       trend: { type: Number, default: 0 }
     })

     const formattedValue = computed(() => {
       // Format logic here
       return new Intl.NumberFormat().format(props.value)
     })
     </script>
     ```

4. Extract business logic into composables:
   - Create reusable composables for data fetching, filtering, and calculations:
     ```javascript
     // useMetricsData.js
     import { ref, computed, onMounted } from 'vue'
     import { metricsService } from '@/services/metricsService'

     export function useMetricsData(timeRange = 'week') {
       const metrics = ref([])
       const isLoading = ref(false)
       const error = ref(null)

       const fetchMetrics = async (range) => {
         isLoading.value = true
         try {
           metrics.value = await metricsService.getMetrics(range)
         } catch (err) {
           error.value = err.message
         } finally {
           isLoading.value = false
         }
       }

       onMounted(() => fetchMetrics(timeRange))

       const totalValue = computed(() => 
         metrics.value.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.value, 0)
       )

       return {
         metrics,
         isLoading,
         error,
         totalValue,
         fetchMetrics
       }
     }
     ```

5. Create service layer for API interactions:
   - Move API calls to dedicated service files:
     ```javascript
     // metricsService.js
     import { apiClient } from '@/api/client'

     export const metricsService = {
       async getMetrics(timeRange) {
         const response = await apiClient.get(`/metrics?range=${timeRange}`)
         return response.data
       },

       async getDashboardData() {
         const response = await apiClient.get('/dashboard')
         return response.data
       }
     }
     ```

6. Refactor Dashboard.vue to use new components:
   - Simplify the main Dashboard.vue to orchestrate components:
     ```vue
     <template>
       <div class="dashboard">
         <dashboard-header 
           :title="title" 
           :date-range="dateRange"
           @range-changed="updateDateRange" 
         />

         <metrics-summary :metrics="metrics" :is-loading="isLoading" />

         <div class="dashboard-content">
           <dashboard-charts :chart-data="chartData" />
           <dashboard-tables :table-data="tableData" />
         </div>
       </div>
     </template>

     <script setup>
     import { ref } from 'vue'
     import DashboardHeader from './components/DashboardHeader.vue'
     import MetricsSummary from './components/MetricsSummary.vue'
     import DashboardCharts from './components/DashboardCharts.vue'
     import DashboardTables from './components/DashboardTables.vue'
     import { useMetricsData } from '@/composables/useMetricsData'
     import { useChartData } from '@/composables/useChartData'

     const title = ref('Dashboard')
     const dateRange = ref('week')

     const updateDateRange = (range) => {
       dateRange.value = range
       fetchMetrics(range)
     }

     const { 
       metrics, 
       isLoading, 
       error, 
       fetchMetrics 
     } = useMetricsData(dateRange.value)

     const { chartData, tableData } = useChartData(metrics)
     </script>
     ```

7. Implement shared state management:
   - Use Pinia store for state that needs to be shared across components:
     ```javascript
     // dashboardStore.js
     import { defineStore } from 'pinia'
     import { metricsService } from '@/services/metricsService'

     export const useDashboardStore = defineStore('dashboard', {
       state: () => ({
         filters: {
           dateRange: 'week',
           category: 'all'
         },
         metrics: [],
         isLoading: false,
         error: null
       }),

       actions: {
         async fetchDashboardData() {
           this.isLoading = true
           try {
             const data = await metricsService.getDashboardData(this.filters)
             this.metrics = data.metrics
           } catch (error) {
             this.error = error.message
           } finally {
             this.isLoading = false
           }
         },

         updateFilters(filters) {
           this.filters = { ...this.filters, ...filters }
           this.fetchDashboardData()
         }
       },

       getters: {
         totalMetrics: (state) => 
           state.metrics.reduce((sum, metric) => sum + metric.value, 0)
       }
     })
     ```

8. Optimize performance:
   - Implement lazy loading for components where appropriate
   - Use memoization for expensive calculations
   - Ensure proper use of v-memo and v-once directives

9. Document the new component architecture:
   - Create documentation explaining component relationships
   - Document props, events, and slots for each component
   - Provide usage examples for composables

